ABL="Ableton/MIDI-friendly: device names map to stock Live devices or generic modules; patterns are plain MIDI clips."
EARLY="If the track has drums, they enter early (~8 counts / ~10-12 s); only pad/texture before that (Matt's preference)."

# 1 ---------------------------------------------- frequency-zone roles + establish/expand/contrast/return (YdnMi_TIXso)
S("YdnMi_TIXso","Beyond the Texture: Advanced Ambient Music Tutorial",
 "ambient-zone-owners-low-a1-body-150-800-focus-air-hand-off-foreground-one-sequence-quiet-noise-minus-20-establish-expand-contrast-return",
 "Ambient 'beyond the 32-bar loop': one owner per frequency zone (bass root at A1, body 150-800 Hz, focal voice, quiet air noise at about -20 dB), depth from level/attack/brightness/reverb, hand off the foreground instead of adding layers, and an establish -> expand -> build contrast -> return arc where the return reveals something new",
 "Delta over existing frequency-layering/role skills: take a competent ambient loop beyond 32 bars by giving each zone exactly one owner (a mono bass parked on the key root, e.g. A1 in A minor, whose only other spectral peaks are octaves/fifths; a mid 'body' layer of two unsynced loops at roughly 150-800 Hz; one focal 'storyteller' voice; a barely audible air/noise layer around -20 dB), shaping foreground vs background with level, attack, brightness and reverb only, handing the foreground to a single new sequence each section instead of stacking layers, and arranging the sections as establish -> expand -> build contrast -> return with something new revealed on the return.",
 ["A DAW with a spectrum analyser (any FFT meter with note readout)","A bass synth, a pad, a pluck synth with delay, a noise source with filter + reverb"],
 [("key","string","Key/root, e.g. A minor (bass sits on A1)",True),("sections","list","Section plan for establish/expand/contrast/return, in 8-bar blocks",True)],
 [("zone_map","notes","Which layer owns low / body / focus / air"),("arrangement","midi","Section clips following the 4-stage arc")],
 ["Balance: never leave the low zone or the air zone empty; a track with lots of highs and no bass (or the reverse) sounds off.",
  "Bass extra peaks should be octaves/fifths of the root (harmonic series), not clashing partials; keep the bass filter in check.",
  "Hide/tuck elements with level and full-spectrum placement, not stereo wideners or hard panning; keep things relatively centred (the source advises no wideners).",
  "The source modulates the noise layer's comb filter with random modulators; that is left out here (steady, no random variation). The noise layer is a soft sustained bed, not crackles or ticks.",
  "Hand off the foreground: one well-made sequence can replace five extra layers.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Set the root and park a simple mono unison bass on it (e.g. A1 for A minor). Check the analyser: its main peak reads A1 and the only other peaks are octaves and the fifth.","Bass synth + analyser","Low zone owned by one part"),
  ("Add the body: a pad or two loops of the same pad playing slightly out of sync, with energy mostly 150-800 Hz.","Pad + analyser","Mid-range foundation that the bass does not fight"),
  ("Decide depth per layer: distant atmosphere (quiet, slow attack, darker, more reverb), supporting pad (medium), focal voice (louder, faster attack, brighter, drier). Use only level, attack, brightness and reverb to place them.","Mixer + envelopes + filter + reverb","Clear foreground/background"),
  ("Bring in one focal melodic voice/loop as the storyteller over the top; it does not need to be high or loud, only clearly in front.","Lead/loop","Listener has something to follow"),
  ("For the next 8-bar section, hand the foreground to one hand-played pluck sequence: simple envelopes, a long slow LFO on cutoff, a rhythmic delay. Do not add other new layers at the same time.","Pluck synth + delay","Section change from one new element"),
  ("Add a quiet air/noise bed: filtered noise (low-pass plus a fixed comb) into a large reverb at about -20 dB. A/B it muted vs on to confirm you feel it more than hear it.","Noise + filters + reverb","Top end filled without being noticed"),
  ("Arrange the sections as establish (body + bass), expand (focal voice), build contrast (new sequence / changed relationships in rhythm, register or voicing), return (home material plus one new element). Fade/introduce each element slowly.","Arrangement","Arc with a purposeful return")],
 ["Analyser: bass fundamental at the chosen root (e.g. A1), with no strong non-octave/non-fifth peaks below 150 Hz.","Low (<150 Hz), body (150-800 Hz) and air (>8 kHz) bands all have energy in every section.","Each new section adds at most one new foreground element.","The return section contains the opening material plus at least one element not in the opening.","Noise layer level is about -20 dB relative to the pad; removing it measurably reduces >8 kHz energy."],
 ["ambient","arrangement","frequency-zones","depth","foreground-handoff","emotional-arc","return","noise-bed"])

# 2 ---------------------------------------------- 4-layer acoustic-style kick (oRhBZ6L9rU8)
S("oRhBZ6L9rU8","Make Acoustic-Sounding Kick Drums in Serum 2 (Realistic & Punchy)",
 "acoustic-style-kick-four-layers-sine-body-pitch-env-phase-offset-transient-osc-faded-in-sample-layer-hp-filtered-short-pink-noise-tail",
 "Acoustic-sounding punchy kick from one three-oscillator synth: sine body with level and pitch envelopes, a tiny high-pitched transient oscillator offset in phase, a one-shot acoustic sample layer faded in after the transient and high-passed, and a short quiet pink-noise tail, then low/mid EQ and fast-attack compression",
 "Build a realistic acoustic-style kick in any synth with three oscillators plus noise: osc A = sine body (random phase 0, phase 90 deg) with a level envelope and a bipolar pitch envelope of about +12 to +24 semitones, tuned down a few semitones; osc B = very short, very high-tuned transient 'click' on the same pitch-envelope shape with phase about 95 deg so it does not stack with the body; osc C = a one-shot acoustic kick sample whose level envelope fades in after the transient and is high-passed on its own filter; plus a gentle, short pink-noise tail; finish with a low-end and top/mid EQ boost and single-band fast-attack compression.",
 ["A synth with 3 oscillators (one able to play a one-shot sample), a noise source, envelopes/LFOs-as-envelopes, a filter routable to one oscillator","An original or free acoustic kick one-shot (no factory-sample copying)"],
 [("tempo","number","Track tempo",False),("kick_sample","audio","Original/free acoustic kick one-shot for the texture layer",True)],
 [("kick_patch","preset","Four-layer kick patch"),("kick_hit","audio","Rendered one-shot")],
 ["Retrigger every oscillator: random phase 0 on the body and transient so each hit is identical (steady, no random variation).",
  "The transient is one short attack per kick, not a texture. Keep it low enough that the kick reads as a thump, and never repeat it as fast ticks (Matt's no-clicks rule).",
  "The noise tail is optional: keep it gentle and short. If it reads as a per-beat hi-hat tick in an ambient track, mute it.",
  "Skip the sample layer's own transient with a fade-in so transients do not stack and get messy.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Osc A body: basic sine, random phase 0, phase 90. Assign envelope 1 to level with a short body decay; assign envelope 2 bipolar to coarse pitch at about +12 to +24 st with a fast drop; tune down a couple of semitones for weight.","Synth osc A + 2 envelopes","Flat thud becomes a kick body"),
  ("Osc B transient: same sine, random phase 0, phase about 95 so the two oscillators do not line up; a very short level envelope, the same pitch-envelope source tuned very high, and the shortest envelope you can draw.","Synth osc B","A small click on the front of the body"),
  ("Osc C texture: one-shot acoustic kick sample. Pick a pleasing scan/start point, give it a level envelope with a short fade-in so its attack is skipped, and route it alone through a high-pass filter to roll off its lows.","Synth osc C sample + filter 2","Acoustic character without a second click or low-end smear"),
  ("Balance the layers: bring the sample layer down until the kick sounds clean, then retune the body pitch and reshape body/transient envelopes to taste (higher body = thumpier).","Mixer","Clean, punchy, acoustic-ish blend"),
  ("Noise tail: short envelope on a pink stereo noise oscillator, low level and short decay, like a faint top on the hit.","Noise osc","A little air after the hit"),
  ("FX: EQ boost on the lows (raise Q a little) and on the top/mids; single-band compressor with fast attack at about half amount, then make up gain.","EQ + compressor","Finished punchy acoustic-style kick")],
 ["Rendered hit: peak in the first 10 ms, then a body whose pitch falls over tens of ms.","Two consecutive hits are sample-identical (no random phase).","Sample layer adds energy after the transient only (its first ms are faded).","In an ambient render the kick enters by ~10-12 s."],
 ["drums","kick","acoustic","layering","pitch-envelope","transient","punchy"])

# 3 ---------------------------------------------- empty-loop seamless drone loop (2faaqrLjTs8)
S("2faaqrLjTs8","The Empty Loop Technique (how to make seamless soundscapes loops)",
 "empty-loop-seamless-drone-loop-record-silent-counted-loop-first-then-overdub-wet-only-reverb-drone-no-seam-gap",
 "Seamless soundscape loop with no audible cut at the seam: set the loop length first with an empty (silent) counted loop, then overdub wet-only long-reverb drone layers on top, so the length comes from the count, not from guessing where an ethereal sound starts and ends",
 "Make a drone/soundscape loop with no gap or cut at the loop point by first recording an empty loop of an exact counted length (e.g. 4 bars of 4 counted while playing silently or at zero volume), then stacking/overdubbing wet-only long-reverb drone passes onto it starting anywhere, so reverb tails wrap across the seam; in a DAW the empty loop is simply a fixed-length bar-locked loop region you record overdubs into.",
 ["A looper (pedal or DAW looper/clip with overdub) or a DAW loop region","A long reverb set 100% wet (dry signal removed) for the drone"],
 [("bars","number","Loop length in bars at the track tempo",True),("source","audio","Guitar, synth or any sustained source for the drone",True)],
 [("seamless_loop","audio","Drone loop with no seam gap")],
 ["Rhythmic picked parts can be looped by count directly; the empty-loop step is for ethereal, attack-less drones where no start/end is audible.",
  "Keep the tempo reference in your hands/head (or the DAW clock) while recording the empty loop; the source counts 1-2-3-4 while playing silently.",
  "Drone = remove the dry signal and keep only a very long reverb.",
  "A seam gap or cut is distracting; also avoid any click at the seam (Matt's no-clicks rule).",ABL],
 [("Set the drone: route the source into a very long reverb with the dry signal removed (100% wet).","Reverb","Ethereal drone with no clear attacks"),
  ("Record an empty loop: keep counting the tempo (play muted or at zero volume) and close the loop on the count, e.g. after 4 bars of 4. In a DAW, just create a fixed-length bar-locked loop clip.","Looper / DAW loop","Silent loop of exact length"),
  ("Overdub (stack) the wet drone onto the empty loop, entering whenever you like at roughly the same tempo.","Looper overdub","Drone fills the loop; tails cross the loop point"),
  ("Listen through several cycles for any cut or gap at the loop point; if none, continue overdubbing more ambient layers on top.","Listening","Seamless, continuous soundscape")],
 ["Loop length equals the counted bar length at the tempo (within a few ms).","No silence gap or level dip >3 dB at the loop boundary across 3 cycles.","No click/transient spike at the seam."],
 ["loops","looper","drone","seamless","overdub","reverb","soundscape","ambient"])

# 4 ---------------------------------------------- autopan-as-gate on a long texture (RDubhupxDsw)
S("RDubhupxDsw","Ableton Live Tutorial - Gated Textures using Autopan",
 "tempo-synced-autopan-phase-360-amplitude-gate-on-long-ambient-texture-shape-softened-edges-reverb-after-gate-amount-as-on-off",
 "Tempo-synced amplitude gate on a long ambient texture made from an auto-pan: beats mode at 1/16, stereo phase raised to 360 deg so left/right move together (on/off volume instead of panning), edge softness set with the shape and waveform controls, then a reverb after the gate to smooth it; amount knob brings the gate in and out",
 "Turn a long ambient texture/soundscape sample into a steady tempo-locked pulsing layer by putting an auto-pan device on it in beats (tempo-sync) mode at 1/16, raising its phase offset to 360 degrees so both channels rise and fall together (a volume gate, the basis of an isochronic-style pulse), shaping the on/off edges with the shape and waveform controls, adding a large-decay reverb after it to soften the gating, and using the amount control to fade the gate in or out of the arrangement.",
 ["A DAW with an auto-pan/tremolo device that has tempo sync, phase offset, shape and waveform controls (or any tremolo/LFO-to-gain)","A long sustained texture or soundscape sample (original/free)","A reverb"],
 [("texture","audio","Long ambient texture, ideally 30 s+",True),("tempo","number","Track BPM; the 1/16 gate rate in Hz = BPM/15",True)],
 [("gated_texture","audio","Tempo-locked pulsing texture"),("gate_automation","automation","Amount automation to bring the gate in/out")],
 ["Phase 360 is what turns panning into gating: with 0 the sound moves left-right; with 360 both sides move together as an on/off volume effect.",
  "Our arithmetic, not from the source: in beats mode a 1/16 gate pulses at BPM/15 Hz (e.g. 120 BPM = 8 Hz, 96 BPM = 6.4 Hz). Choose tempo and division to land on the wanted pulse rate.",
  "No clicks (Matt's preference): use a rounded/sine-like waveform and soften the shape so on/off edges are not hard; keep the reverb after the gate. Avoid square edges at fast rates.",
  "The source shows no rate ramp. For an isochronic rate ramp, automate tempo-free Hz rate instead (still an open gap, to be written in-house).",
  "No health claims: describe pulse rates as target ranges only.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Drop the long texture on an audio track (an intro or breakdown element) and add an auto-pan; turn its amount up to hear it move left and right.","Audio track + auto-pan","Plain auto-pan movement"),
  ("Switch the auto-pan to beats (tempo-sync) mode and set the rate to 1/16.","Auto-pan rate","Movement locked to the track tempo"),
  ("Raise the phase offset to 360 degrees.","Auto-pan phase","Left and right now pulse together: an on/off volume gate"),
  ("Fine-tune the gate edges with the shape control and the waveform selector (rounded/sine-like for soft pulses).","Auto-pan shape/waveform","Gate with the wanted edge softness"),
  ("Add a reverb after the auto-pan and raise its decay for a large room/hall, to soften the gating.","Reverb","Smoother, pulsing ambient layer"),
  ("Play it with the beat and automate the auto-pan amount down/up to take the gate off and bring it back between sections.","Amount automation","Gate in perfect sync with tempo, switchable per section")],
 ["Left and right channels' amplitude envelopes are in phase (correlation of L/R envelopes ~1).","Envelope pulse rate equals BPM/15 Hz for a 1/16 setting.","No transient spikes at gate edges (HF spike counter stays low).","With amount at 0 the texture is ungated."],
 ["isochronic","gate","tremolo","autopan","tempo-sync","texture","ambient","pulse"])
